<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sen no Rikyū</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sen no Rikyu, Sen Rikyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sen no Rikyū was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master from a merchant family of Sakai who shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-tea-ceremony">tea ceremony</a> into its classic form. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, for whom he organised great tea gatherings, and promoted wabi, a rustic and simple aesthetic. In 1591 he fell from Hideyoshi's favour for reasons still debated and was ordered to commit ritual suicide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu.jpg" alt="Sen no Rikyū" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: painted by 長谷川等伯, calligraphy by 春屋宗園 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sen_no_Rikyu_JPN_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
